{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvp8WL4tnYKarb5tZd2R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the new policy as `policies/dont-take-the-lazy-path.md`:\n\n> # Don't take the lazy path\n>\n> Solve the task in the best way, not the least-effort way. Never pick or recommend a weaker option because the better one costs more code, tokens, or work, and never quietly ship a partial version — finish it or say plainly what is left.\n> This is about doing the asked work fully and well, not about expanding scope or writing longer replies.\n\nI read \"no matter the token or effort cost\" as \"cost alone never justifies a worse solution\", not \"spend as much effort as possible\". If you meant the stronger version, the wording would need to change.\n\nThe last line is there on purpose. The system-wide prompt says to prefer small changes and not do things you weren't asked to do, and another policy says to keep replies short. Without that line, \"don't be lazy\" could be read as permission to grow the task or write longer answers.\n\nI checked it against the system-wide prompt, the project rules and the other policies. Nothing conflicts or repeats, so no rewording was needed. The file is 7 lines, about the same length as the others.\n\nOne possible follow-up that I left alone because it was out of scope: \"Prefer small, focused changes\" in `prompts/meta.md` could itself be read as \"prefer low effort\". It might be worth rewording to make clear it means small in scope. This is noted in `implementation.md`.\n\nBoth tasks are committed and the worktree is clean."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1069,"cache_read_input_tokens":19350,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1069},"output_tokens":463,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"inp